WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.549
On the morning of April 3rd, 2011,

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.139
the renowned artist Ai Weiwei was

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.699
picked up by Chinese police at an

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.409
airport in Beijing.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.909
Later that day, he found himself in

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.619
the back of a van surrounded by

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.179
security officials and wearing a

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.349
black hood.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.729
They took him to a secret detention

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.529
facility where he was interrogated

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.939
and monitored.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.739
It was here that I began to think of

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.599
his young son, wondering when he

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.559
would see him again.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.019
I was thinking, I'll be in that

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.269
position for the next

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.010
10 or certain years.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.079
That's what they told me.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.749
The memory it only belongs

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.299
to isolated

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.300
the men.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.339
And I tried to

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.409
capture what happened

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.270
in the past.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.129
And I realize if

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.989
there's a chance, I will write

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.149
that down so

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.279
I can pass to my son, and

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.189
at that time he was two years old,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.254
so he would have a full record.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.959
After 81 days and following an

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.848
international outcry, Ai was

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:06.799
released by Chinese authorities, but

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.389
he was far from free.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.009
They held onto his passport so he

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.419
couldn't leave the country, and they

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.489
continue to watch his every move.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.968
It was then that he began to write.

00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:16.879
The resulting book "One

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.889
Thousand Years of Joys and Sorrows"

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.019
tells the story of his detention,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.309
his repeated struggles with the

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.049
communist government, and how what

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.579
happened to his father, the famed

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.559
poet Ai Quing during China's

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.389
Cultural Revolution, shaped

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.769
his worldview.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.689
Why art is necessary

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.039
and important.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.749
Why a freedom of speech are

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.369
necessary.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.439
So all those issues are not

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.169
just come to me, but rather

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.059
come from my father's

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.060
generation.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.199
He was heavily punished

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.389
with about half million

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.739
of the intellectuals

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.009
and the mayor, and the first

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.709
generation is sent to the

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.229
reeducation camp,

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.089
then relocated us from

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.429
the Capitol to

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.769
the most remote province,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.448
Shinto.

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:10.698
And that experience

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.680
gave me early.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.409
Very intimate

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.449
relations to politics.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.279
Growing up in exile and watching

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.989
his father struggle profoundly

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.299
affected his views on human rights,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.039
individuality and freedom of

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:26.969
expression.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.379
And he would later explore these

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.089
themes in his art.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.159
In 2005, I turned his fingers

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.719
from the camera lens to the computer

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.819
keyboard and typed his first blog

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.649
post, a move that had

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.179
profound implications for his

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.180
career.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.239
At that moment, neither I nor

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.649
China's internet censorship

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.539
apparatus anticipated quite

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.159
what a transformational act this

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.929
would turn out to be for me, he

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.009
writes.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.449
I entered the public's field of

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.979
vision with the force of a bullet

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.029
from a gun.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.919
If you have art exhibition, you

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.019
may have one maybe 100,000

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.779
people went to those exhibitions.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.969
But if you put a social media

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.859
post immediately

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.969
can be saying a

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.969
hundred thousand people

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.799
a day, as you know,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.330
it's it's very

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.209
convenient.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.459
I also felt liberated.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.049
Every character that I tapped on my

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.789
keyboard was emblematic of a new

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.589
kind of freedom, he writes.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.449
By enabling alternative voices,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.039
the internet weakened the power of

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.319
autocracy, dispelling the obstacles

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.879
it tried to put in the individual's

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.019
way.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.849
I harness the power of the internet

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.039
to push for government transparency.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.989
Broadcast is anti-authoritarian

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.909
views and advocate for change

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.499
in China and abroad.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.119
Eventually, government censors shut

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.019
him down.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.849
His blog, along with thousands of

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.009
his photographs, disappeared

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.839
even in online search within China

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.639
for his name, drew a blank.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.749
Which foreshadowed his own

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.550
disappearance a few years later.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:55.919
These Olympics, as you know, are a

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.749
hugely controversial why

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.519
China's been widely criticized for

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.379
human rights abuses, including the

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.879
mistreatment of pro-democracy

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.129
activists and ethnic minorities.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.569
As the world watches the Beijing

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.369
Olympics, American politicians have

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.199
become more vocal in their criticism

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.419
of China's record on human rights.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.069
Ai says these gestures will have

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.598
little impact

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:17.909
When West talk about transparency

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.799
and human rights, and

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.838
the China is not going to listen to

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.679
it because in the West,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.559
you cannot even defended

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.049
the value of human rights.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.939
If you talk of a United States,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.009
you have so many black people

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.149
in jail.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.549
I mean, you can't has the biggest

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.679
population in jail

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.359
within the world, and the U.S.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.189
supports so many,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.619
you know, bad society

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.899
cells and arms and

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.719
do all kind of dirty works.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.669
How can you talk about

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.849
human rights under transparency

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.769
while you hold Julian Assange, a

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.599
journalist could be

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.639
facing 175 years

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.629
in jail?

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.449
How can talk about price freedom?

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.999
So this kind of argument,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.799
you know, it's not going to work.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.509
I sees the struggle between China

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.399
and America continuing and remains

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.899
deeply troubled by the direction of

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.189
both countries.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.839
He recently drew headlines for

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.279
comparing the state of free speech

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.749
in America to the Cultural

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.339
Revolution.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.348
In many ways, you already

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.629
in the authoritarian state.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.649
You just don't know it.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:27.850
How so?

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.449
Many sins happens today in

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.399
US is can

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.329
be compared.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.519
Cultural Revolution in China.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.069
Like what?

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.959
Like people trying to

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.089
be unified

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.359
in a certain political correctness

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.849
that is very dangerous.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.289
These thoughts are echoed in his

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:50.669
memoirs.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.709
"Ideological cleansing," he writes,

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.109
"exists not only under totalitarian

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:55.979
regimes.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.509
It is also present in a different

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.879
form in liberal western democracies.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.349
Under the influence of politically

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:02.519
correct extremism.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.259
Individual thought and expression

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.419
are too often curbed and too often

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.999
replaced by empty political slogans.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.769
It is not hard to find examples

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.539
today of people saying and doing

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.399
things they don't believe in, simply

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.929
to fall in line with the prevailing

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.639
narrative and make a superficial

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.469
public statement."

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.149
Tellingly, though, Ai Weiwei's book

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.559
is readily available in liberal

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.269
Western democracies, where it's

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.829
received widespread acclaim

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.560
in China, it's censored.

